        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Ceiling Painting Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ceilings are lit from a harsh angle and viewed from directly beneath, which means they show every flaw a wall would hide. Most bad ceilings come from a handful of avoidable errors.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Wall Paint on the Ceiling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ceiling paint is formulated thicker and flatter than wall paint precisely because it has to cling to an overhead surface and diffuse light without highlighting texture. Wall paint drips more, covers in fewer, thinner coats, and its slight sheen picks out every seam and roller edge.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing Anything With Sheen</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Eggshell or satin on a ceiling reflects light across the surface at a glancing angle, turning minor drywall imperfections, taped seams, and roller lap marks into visible defects. Flat is the correct finish for almost every ceiling, and the small cleanability penalty is worth it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stopping and Restarting Mid-Ceiling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ceiling paint sets quickly, and rolling into an edge that has begun to dry leaves a permanent lap mark straight across the room. Work in overlapping strips across the short dimension and keep a wet edge from wall to wall — finish the whole ceiling in one session, not in stages.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting Over Stains Without Priming</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water stains, smoke, and tannin bleed straight through latex ceiling paint, often not appearing until the paint has dried and you have already done two coats. Spot-prime any discolouration with a stain-blocking primer first — ordinary paint will not seal it, no matter how many coats go on.
              </p>
            </div>
